04:58:22.120 --> 04:58:23.612
>> RUTH ASSAD WAS RUTH ASAWA WAS ONE OF THE MOST ADMIRED

04:58:23.612 --> 04:58:26.749
ARTISTS EDUCATORS AND CIVIC LEADERS OF THE 20TH CENTURY.

04:58:26.749 --> 04:58:30.219
BORN IN 1926, IN NORWALK, CALIFORNIA, SHE WENT ON TO

04:58:30.219 --> 04:58:33.156
BUILD A SIX DECADE CAREER THAT SPANNED DRAWING, PAINTING,

04:58:33.156 --> 04:58:36.559
PRINTMAKING, BRONZE CASTING, PUBLIC ART AND OF COURSE THE

04:58:36.559 --> 04:58:40.229
ICONIC LOOP TROIS SCULPTURES THAT MADE HER WORLD RENOWNED

04:58:40.229 --> 04:58:41.231
WHILE SHE WAS BASED IN SAN FRANCISCO.

04:58:41.231 --> 04:58:44.367
HER INFLUENCE EXTENDED FAR BEYOND OUR CITY.

04:58:44.367 --> 04:58:45.969
HER WORK IS CELEBRATED AROUND THE WORLD AND TODAY IT IS

04:58:45.969 --> 04:58:48.905
FEATURED IN THE LARGEST EXHIBITION EVER DEDICATED TO A

04:58:48.905 --> 04:58:51.907
WOMAN ARTIST AT THE MUSEUM OF MODERN ART IN NEW YORK.

04:58:51.907 --> 04:58:53.443
>> BUT SAN FRANCISCO WAS HER HOME AND HER LEGACY IS DEEPLY

04:58:53.443 --> 04:58:57.670
WOVEN INTO THE FABRIC OF OUR CITY FROM FOUNTAINS AND MURALS

04:58:57.670 --> 04:58:59.736
TO SCULPTURES THAT REMAIN PUBLICLY ACCESSIBLE TO THIS DAY

04:58:59.736 --> 04:59:03.539
TO OUR VERY OWN RUTH ASAWA SCHOOL OF THE ARTS.

04:59:03.539 --> 04:59:07.543
RUTH RUTH'S ALWAYS LIFE STORY IS ALSO A STORY OF RESILIENCE.

04:59:07.543 --> 04:59:08.945
SHE WAS THE FOURTH OF SEVEN CHILDREN BORN TO JAPANESE

04:59:08.945 --> 04:59:12.150
IMMIGRANT FARMERS AND ARE JUST 16 YEARS OLD.

04:59:12.150 --> 04:59:13.483
SHE WAS FORCIBLY REMOVED FROM HER HOME AND INCARCERATED BY

04:59:13.483 --> 04:59:16.153
THE UNITED STATES GOVERNMENT DURING WORLD WAR TWO.

04:59:16.153 --> 04:59:19.690
EVEN UNDER THOSE HARSH, HARSH CONDITIONS SHE FOUND WAYS TO

04:59:19.690 --> 04:59:23.930
CREATE BY TAKING ART LESSONS FROM FELLOW DETAINEES AFTER THE

04:59:23.930 --> 04:59:24.427
WAR DESPITE FACING DISCRIMINATION THAT BLOCKED HER

04:59:24.427 --> 04:59:27.830
FROM BECOMING A STUDENT TEACHER, SHE CONTINUED HER

04:59:27.830 --> 04:59:30.634
EDUCATION AT BLACK MOUNTAIN COLLEGE WHERE SHE STUDIED

04:59:30.634 --> 04:59:32.350
ALONGSIDE LEADING ARTISTS AND DEVELOPED THE VALUES THAT

04:59:32.350 --> 04:59:35.372
WOULD SHAPE HER LIFELONG ADVOCACY FOR ACCESSIBLE ARTS

04:59:35.372 --> 04:59:39.242
EDUCATION THAT COMMITMENT DEFINED SO MUCH OF HER WORK

04:59:39.242 --> 04:59:42.111
HERE IN SAN FRANCISCO. IN 1968 SHE FOUNDED THE

04:59:42.111 --> 04:59:45.382
ALVARADO SCHOOL ARTS WORKSHOP BRINGING PROFESSIONAL ARTISTS

04:59:45.382 --> 04:59:49.119
AND PARENTS INTO PUBLIC SCHOOLS TO TEACH CHILDREN THROUGH HANDS

04:59:49.119 --> 04:59:51.654
ON ART MAKING. AT ITS HEIGHT, THE PROGRAM

04:59:51.654 --> 04:59:53.123
REACHED MORE THAN 50 SCHOOLS AND BECAME A NATIONAL MODEL FOR

04:59:53.123 --> 04:59:55.659
ARTS, EDUCATION AND PUBLIC SERVICE PROGRAMS ACROSS THE

04:59:55.659 --> 04:59:56.593
COUNTRY. ASAWA ALSO SERVED ON SAN

04:59:56.593 --> 05:00:00.263
FRANCISCO'S ARTS COMMISSION NATIONAL THE CALIFORNIA ARTS

05:00:00.263 --> 05:00:03.199
COUNCIL, THE NATIONAL ENDOWMENT FOR THE ARTS AND AS A TRUSTEE

05:00:03.199 --> 05:00:04.267
OF THE FINE ARTS MUSEUMS OF SAN FRANCISCO.

05:00:04.267 --> 05:00:07.537
AND SHE PLAYED A KEY ROLE IN FOUNDING SAN FRANCISCO'S

05:00:07.537 --> 05:00:10.273
PUBLIC ARTS HIGH SCHOOL WHICH PROUDLY BEARS HER NAME TODAY

05:00:10.273 --> 05:00:13.377
